WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.680
Whatever happened to Madam Hooch? Ron Weasley's&nbsp;
brother Charlie? Here's a look at some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.760
many "Harry Potter" storylines that just&nbsp;
completely disappeared from the films.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.800
Madam Hooch appears in "Harry Potter and the&nbsp;
Sorcerer's Stone," in a small but significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.080
moment for the Boy Who Lived. Played by Zoe&nbsp;
Wanamaker, Hogwarts' resident Quidditch coach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.960
teaches flying lessons to the first-year students.&nbsp;
Professor McGonagall observes Harry's natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.760
prowess in the air, and gives him a position&nbsp;
as Seeker for Gryffindor's Quidditch team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.920
Madam Hooch can be seen refereeing&nbsp;
Harry's first match against Slytherin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.720
where he almost swallows the Golden Snitch.&nbsp;
And then… that's the last we ever see of her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.360
even though the character&nbsp;
continues throughout the books.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.840
Why? Well, actor Zoe Wanamaker&nbsp;
only signed on for one film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.080
which is likely why Madam Hooch disappears&nbsp;
from the story after "Sorcerer's Stone."

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.400
Another character to only feature in one film in&nbsp;
the "Harry Potter" series is Gilderoy Lockhart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.520
Harry's second Defense Against The Dark&nbsp;
Arts professor is, arguably, his most inept.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.560
Appearing in "Harry Potter and the&nbsp;
Chamber of Secrets," Lockhart is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.920
charismatic, charming celebrity, renowned for&nbsp;
successfully banishing various dark creatures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.760
But he's all style and no substance. His lessons&nbsp;
at Hogwarts expose him as a fraud, and he admits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.000
at the end of the film that he's taken credit&nbsp;
for other people's work throughout his career.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.320
"Chamber of Secrets" culminates with&nbsp;
a Memory Charm backfiring on Lockhart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.880
thanks to Ron's broken wand. Lockhart gets a&nbsp;
taste of his own medicine, losing all memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.360
of who he is. And then, you guessed it, he&nbsp;
disappears for the rest of the movie series.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.240
"Hello. Who are you?”

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:38.960
"Ron Weasley."

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.120
"Really? And, uh, who am I?"

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.320
In the books, Harry, Ron and Hermione run&nbsp;
into an amnesiac Lockhart in St. Mungo's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.640
Hospital For Magical Maladies and Injuries&nbsp;
during "Harry Potter and the Order of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.760
Phoenix," but actor Kenneth Branagh likely wasn't&nbsp;
available to film this cameo for the movies.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.240
"Sorcerer's Stone" and "Chamber of&nbsp;
Secrets" includes several scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.960
showing Harry's school lessons. From&nbsp;
"Prisoner of Azkaban" onwards, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:12.400
Harry's classes take more of a backseat in favor&nbsp;
of the growing drama at Hogwarts and beyond.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.280
The boy wizard's lessons tend to appear&nbsp;
only when something significant happens:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.160
Harry's flight on Buckbeak in "Prisoner of&nbsp;
Azkaban," Moody explaining the Unforgivable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:27.680
Curses in "Goblet of Fire," etc. The books have&nbsp;
more space to explore Harry's journey, but in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.880
films Harry uses several spells seemingly out&nbsp;
of the blue with no prior classroom practice.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.720
"Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire"&nbsp;
is a turning point in the story for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.640
everyone's favorite teenage trio. Lord&nbsp;
Voldemort makes his comeback at last,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.560
emerging from a cauldron with no nose&nbsp;
and a thirst for blood, while Harry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.520
Ron, and Hermione come of age as the&nbsp;
series takes a more dramatic, adult tone.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.200
But it's not all bad for the&nbsp;
Golden Trio, especially Hermione,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.640
who has a whirlwind romance with Quidditch&nbsp;
sensation and Durmstrang student Viktor Krum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.120
which ends with Krum asking Hermione to&nbsp;
write him after he leaves. In the books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.600
this happens, but in the movies, Krum is almost&nbsp;
entirely absent for the rest of the series.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.520
Like Hermione, Harry has a love&nbsp;
interest in "Goblet of Fire":&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.080
Cho Chang, who initially rebuffs Harry&nbsp;
for Cedric Diggory. After Cedric dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.200
however, Harry grows close to Cho&nbsp;
during "Order of the Phoenix."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.440
Harry shares his first kiss with Cho, before&nbsp;
their budding relationship quickly goes awry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:29.200
when she spills the beans to Dolores Umbridge&nbsp;
about Harry's secret group, Dumbledore's Army.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.600
Post-"Order of the Phoenix," Cho graduates&nbsp;
from Hogwarts and Harry pursues a relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.520
with Ginny Weasley. The two are briefly&nbsp;
reunited in "Deathly Hallows" during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.920
prelude to the Battle of Hogwarts. The film&nbsp;
follows a similar path to the books here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.240
reducing Cho to a cameo character&nbsp;
after her abrupt break-up with Harry.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.720
The "Harry Potter" films were mostly&nbsp;
faithful adaptations of the book series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.800
though many characters had to be cut during&nbsp;
the jump from the page to the big screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.960
Multiple members of Hogwarts' faculty&nbsp;
never - or barely - appeared in the films:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.160
Professor Binns, Professor Grubbly-Plank, plus&nbsp;
most of the stuff with the centaur to name a few.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.480
One teacher who did have a cameo appearance -&nbsp;
before disappearing from the film series - is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:14.160
Professor Sinistra. Sinistra teaches Astronomy&nbsp;
lessons throughout Harry's time at Hogwarts. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.600
the film, she is played by an unidentified actress&nbsp;
in an uncredited role, which was later retconned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.720
as Professor Sinistra when the actress' likeness&nbsp;
was used in the "LEGO Harry Potter” video games.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.360
Omitting Professor Sinistra from the other "Harry&nbsp;
Potter" movies was a missed opportunity for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.480
series in regards to representation. The Astronomy&nbsp;
teacher's physical appearance isn't given in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.040
books, though the character was portrayed&nbsp;
by a Black woman during "Sorcerer's Stone."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.920
In hindsight, the films could have further&nbsp;
developed Sinistra as a prominent POC character.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:50.320
Ah, Colin Creevey. Equal parts endearing and&nbsp;
annoying. The star-struck student appears in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:55.360
"Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets,"&nbsp;
as the Boy Who Lived's number one fan. Colin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:00.320
is one of the students to fall victim to&nbsp;
the Basilisk. Thankfully, Harry saves the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.280
day by venturing into the Chamber of Secrets to&nbsp;
rescue Ginny Weasley and kill the giant snake.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.160
"Chamber of Secrets" is the only installment in&nbsp;
the "Harry Potter" series to star Colin Creevey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:15.440
In later films, he is replaced by an original&nbsp;
character called Nigel Wolpert. Nigel can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.120
seen as an amalgam of Colin's character, as well&nbsp;
as his brother, Dennis, who appears in the books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.080
Nigel has a happier ending in "Harry Potter and&nbsp;
the Deathly Hallows Part Two" than his on-page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.800
counterpart: in the books, Colin is killed by&nbsp;
Death Eaters during the Battle of Hogwarts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.440
It seems that Colin's death was instead&nbsp;
given to Lavender Brown, who in the films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.040
is killed by Fenrir Greyback, despite&nbsp;
surviving the battle in the books.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.400
"Prisoner of Azkaban" features some big&nbsp;
changes for Harry, Ron and Hermione, as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.240
face their third year at Hogwarts. New lessons are&nbsp;
introduced, such as Professor Trelawney's study of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:55.520
Divination, and Care of Magical Creatures classes&nbsp;
led by Hagrid. Both classes prove difficult in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.960
different ways: Harry rides a hippogriff in&nbsp;
his first lesson with Hagrid, while Trelawney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:06.320
frequently predicts Harry's imminent death&nbsp;
after seeing the Grim during a Divination class.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.720
Buckbeak, the hippogriff Harry flies on,&nbsp;
plays a small but significant role in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.040
third installment. Draco Malfoy purposely&nbsp;
provokes the beast into attacking him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.160
and Buckbeak is sentenced to death thanks to&nbsp;
Draco's father, Lucius. Thanks to the Time-Turner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:24.320
Harry and Hermione manage to rescue&nbsp;
Buckbeak before his extermination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:29.040
Harry saves Sirius Black from the Dementors,&nbsp;
before his godfather departs with the hippogriff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.840
both of them fugitives from the Ministry of Magic.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.080
In the films, Buckbeak's story ends there, but&nbsp;
in the books there's a whole storyline with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.480
Buckbeak that ends with a moving reunion&nbsp;
with Hagrid. Too bad we never saw it.

00:06:40.636 --> 00:06:44.320
The final installment in the "Harry&nbsp;
Potter" book gives fans their first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.000
look at Albus Dumbledore's childhood&nbsp;
and subsequent, previously unknown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.040
history. Evil journalist Rita Skeeter swoops&nbsp;
in shortly after Dumbledore's death to drag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.240
the Headmaster's name through the dirt in her&nbsp;
revealing biography, "The Life And Lies Of Albus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:03.280
Dumbledore." When Harry reads an excerpt in the&nbsp;
Daily Prophet, it triggers a crisis of faith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:08.640
as he is forced to confront the possibility that&nbsp;
he never knew his mentor's true nature after all.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.920
Skeeter's biography casts a shadow on Dumbledore's&nbsp;
legacy ... but it isn't necessarily untrue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.640
We learn in the book that Dumbledore was involved&nbsp;
with the Dark wizard Grindelwald, as Harry learns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:24.400
from Dumbledore's brother, Aberforth. Aberforth&nbsp;
explains that their sister Ariana was attacked by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:28.960
three Muggle boys in her youth, causing her&nbsp;
magic to spiral out of control. Her father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.920
Percival, went after the Muggles in retribution,&nbsp;
earning him a life sentence in Azkaban and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:39.600
sour reputation for his family. Ariana was later&nbsp;
left in Albus' care after their mother's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:44.320
but she was killed accidentally during a&nbsp;
confrontation between Albus and Grindelwald.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.640
However, almost none of this is explained in&nbsp;
the films. Instead, there's just a brief scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:53.920
that hints at these events - events that Harry&nbsp;
explicitly isn't interested in at all in the film.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:57.840
"I'm not interested in what happened&nbsp;
between you and your brother."

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.480
Ron's older brother, Charlie, has the biggest&nbsp;
blink-and-you'll-miss-it cameo appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:06.720
in the film series, popping up briefly in a&nbsp;
photograph of the Weasley family on their trip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:12.400
to Egypt in "Prisoner of Azkaban." Besides that?&nbsp;
Charlie is an absent member of the Weasley clan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.560
never appearing properly on-screen, though&nbsp;
Ron mentions Charlie in "Harry Potter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:21.040
the Sorcerer's Stone" while explaining that&nbsp;
his brother works with dragons in Romania.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:27.200
"That's a Norwegian Ridgeback! My brother&nbsp;
Charlie works with these in Romania!"

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:29.520
Charlie shows up a few times in the books, though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.400
The wayward Weasley takes Hagrid's baby dragon,&nbsp;
Norbert, off their hands in "Sorcerer's Stone,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:39.040
before returning to Hogwarts in "Goblet of&nbsp;
Fire" as a handler for the dragons used in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.680
the Triwizard Tournament's first task. Like&nbsp;
the rest of his family, Charlie is present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.760
at the final battle against Voldemort in&nbsp;
"Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows."

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.280
His exclusion from the later movies&nbsp;
suggests that his character could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.040
have been retconned out of existence to&nbsp;
allow the film to focus on portraying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.320
other Weasley siblings like&nbsp;
Ginny, Bill, and Fred and George.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:03.520
In "Goblet of Fire," future "Doctor Who"&nbsp;
star David Tennant became one of the long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:07.840
list of acclaimed British actors to feature&nbsp;
in the "Harry Potter" series. Playing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:12.960
villainous Barty Crouch Jr., Tennant's role in&nbsp;
the film was altered from the source material:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:18.000
In the books, when Professor Moody is revealed&nbsp;
to actually be Barty Crouch's son, disguised via&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:23.840
the power of Polyjuice Potion, Barty Crouch Jr.&nbsp;
regales Harry with an in-depth explanation of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:29.520
he was broken out of Azkaban by his parents, only&nbsp;
to reunite with Voldemort and help him enact his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:35.920
plan to capture Harry. Barty's tale concludes with&nbsp;
him receiving the Dementor's Kiss for his crimes.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:40.720
The revelation that Mad-Eye Moody is really&nbsp;
Barty Crouch Jr. is glossed over pretty quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:46.000
in the film, though. Crouch never explains his&nbsp;
motivations, or receives the Dementor's Kiss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.320
Dumbledore remarks that the Dementors&nbsp;
"will find Azkaban's missing prisoner,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:56.160
and Crouch promises that he will "be welcomed&nbsp;
back like a hero." Did Crouch return to Azkaban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:01.760
after that? Or did the Dementors perform their&nbsp;
soul-stealing Kiss off-screen? It isn't clear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:07.120
Crouch's storyline, like other characters in the&nbsp;
films, disappears without closure or explanation.

